  "response": "I ran the context command and read the file. One note: `crimes context` resolved the repo root up to the sibling copy at `examples/messy-ts-app` rather than `evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app`, but the per-file finding set is identical to the scan JSON above (8 findings on `src/billing.ts` — 1 high, 5 medium, 2 low), so the picture is the same.\n\n## What `src/billing.ts` does\n\nIt's the fixture's billing domain module (311 lines, no imports, four exports):\n\n- **Types** — `User`, `InvoiceLine`, `Invoice` declared locally (lines 4–31).\n- **`calculateRefundPreview(user, invoice)`** (40–44) — *awaits* `saveRefundAudit` and `sendRefundEmail`, then returns `invoice.totalCents`. It is not a preview.\n- **`generateInvoice(user, items, paymentMethod?)`** (50–253) — the centre of gravity. One body doing: id/metadata construction, per-plan line pricing (`pro` 5% / `team` 10% / `enterprise` 20%, with `addon_` SKUs exempt), loyalty discount by account age, promo-code resolution (`WELCOME10/20`, `BLACKFRIDAY`, `REF_*`), inline fraud scoring → `allow/review/block`, country tax table, currency selection, invoice assembly, a PII-leaking `console.log` audit line, metric emission, email-body composition, and a 3-attempt retry loop.\n- **`tax(country, subtotalCents)`** (257–268) — the same country→rate table as lines 146–154, as a second copy.\n- **`recommendUpgrade(user)`** (272–305) — four-deep nested plan/tenure/country policy, recomputing `daysSince` the same way `generateInvoice` does.\n- **`listRecentInvoices()`** (307–310) — a stub returning `[]`.\n\n## Files I'd read before editing\n\n`crimes context` reports `likely_tests: [\"src/billing.test.ts\"]` and ranks related files, but the scan's cross-file findings are the sharper signal:\n\n| File | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `src/api/state.ts` | **Highest priority.** Holds rival `User` and `Invoice` interfaces (`crime_00010`, `crime_00011`, both high). |\n| `src/billing.test.ts` | The only test covering this file — and both of its tests are flagged (see below). |\n| `src/api/plan.ts`, `src/jobs/plan-sync.ts`, `src/ui/pricing.ts` | Share the bare `\"enterprise\"` literal with `billing.ts:7` (`crime_00004`) — plan-tier edits must land in all four. |\n| `src/routes/settings/billing.tsx` | UI surface for this domain; carries its own `route_metadata_drift` (`crime_00006`). |\n| `docs/billing.md` | Prose contract for the same domain; also part of the `tenant`-alias drift (`crime_00020`). |\n\nWithin the file itself, nothing is imported — but `tax()` and the inline tax table at 146–154, and the two `daysSince` computations at 92 and 273, are duplicates you must edit together.\n\n## Risks the scan flagged\n\n**Blockers for a careless edit:**\n\n- **Contract Split-Brain × 2, severity high** (`crime_00010`, `crime_00011`). `Invoice` here declares 11 fields; `src/api/state.ts:9` declares 2. Six disagreements — `createdAt`, `currency`, `discountCents`, `subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, `userId` all absent on the API side. `User` likewise disagrees on `createdAt`. Evidence: *\"Nothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other.\"* Adding a field to `Invoice` here will silently not reach the API type.\n- **God Function, severity high** (`crime_00036`): `generateInvoice` is *\"204 lines long… 3.4× the domain function threshold (60 lines)\"*. Per the auto-fix policy I won't split it as a drive-by — flag it, propose separately.\n\n**Traps that mislead the reader:**\n\n- **False Identity, medium** (`crime_00015`): `calculateRefundPreview` — *\"name prefix suggests a pure transformation; side-effect-like calls: saveRefundAudit, sendRefundEmail\"*. Don't call it speculatively, memoise it, or move it.\n- **Logic in the Alibi, medium** (`crime_00016`, L39): the comment *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves\"* is a rule with **no code behind it** — *\"domain terms not found nearby: owner, plan, billing, refund\"*.\n- **Commented-Out Corpse, medium** (`crime_00021`, L33–37): a disabled legacy-refund snippet. Note `crimes context` shows a suppression on `commented_out_code::src/billing.ts::` with `matches_current_finding: false` — the old suppression no longer covers this finding.\n- **Catch and Release, low** (`crime_00032`, L245–247): the retry loop's `catch` is comment-only, declares no binding, and has *\"no rethrow… no logging, telemetry… no typed error result\"*. The function returns a successful-looking `Invoice` whether or not the email went out.\n\n**Testing and determinism:**\n\n- **Both tests are hollow.** `crime_00028`: *\"renders the enterprise plan\"* has *\"0 expect/assert calls\"*; `crime_00034`: *\"creates an invoice\"* uses only weak matchers. `test_gap` percentile is bottom-quartile. Treat this file as effectively untested — I'd add real assertions alongside any behavioural change.\n- **Temporal Recklessness, medium** (`crime_00014`): 7 clock reads at 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273 — *\"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading\"*, so lower-risk than a branching clock, but pinning behaviour in a test needs a fixed clock. The `Math.random()` id at L56 is the same problem for reproducibility.\n\n**Lower-priority context:** God File (`crime_00039`, 311 lines vs a 300 threshold) and 4 TODO/FIXME/HACK/XXX markers (`crime_00042`).\n\n`crimes context` returns `risk.level: \"high\"` for this file. My reading: the single most dangerous thing about editing it is the split-brain `Invoice`/`User` contracts — a type change made here looks complete and isn't. Tell me what you want changed and I'll scope the edit against that.",
